{
  "type": "introduction",
  "content": {
    "tiles": [
      {
        "id": "TILEIso4ZRukGTFz",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p>In any problem that calls for a linear mode that is an equation, the goal is to find the values of <em>m</em> and <em>b</em> for the linear equation <em>y</em> = <em>mx </em>+<em> b </em>that fits the data pattern well. Recall from *Moving Straight Ahead* unit that for linear relationships as <em>x </em>increases by a fixed increment <em>y</em> changes by a constant amount, <em>m</em>, called the rate of change. The y-intercept is (0, <em>b</em>) or <em>b</em>. </p>"
          ]
        }
      },
      [
        {
          "id": "hGG5RWax7HtcWkVc",
          "content": {
            "type": "Table",
            "columnWidths": {
              "7wbCJV5zaNcIaD1D": 80,
              "0TnxVxWd0E0926zJ": 80
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "TILEDpVgiQLXPRCc",
          "title": "y = mx +b",
          "content": {
            "type": "Image",
            "url": "twmm/images/TWMM_2_3_intro_graph_450w.png"
          }
        }
      ],
      {
        "id": "TILEu33tWl4pmKoP",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p>The change in x from one point to the another is also called the *horizontal change* or **run** change. The change in y from one point to another is called the *vertical change* or **rise** change.  The ratio of rise to run is called the **slope** of the line which is also the coefficient of *m* in the equation, y = *m*x + *b*.</p>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "TILEgNrB6P5W_tyV",
        "content": {
          "type": "Image",
          "url": "twmm/images/TWMM_2_3_slope_formula_400w.png"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "TILE2MNmLzG1xm7e",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p>This ratio, slope, describes the steepness of a line that represents the linear function. The equation y = *m*x + *b* is also called the **slope intercept** form of a linear equation. To measure the steepness of a linear equation graph, it helps to imagine a staircase that lies underneath the line.</p>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "TILED4i1EL_qbSsO",
        "content": {
          "type": "Image",
          "url": "twmm/images/TWMM_2_3_staircase_400w.png"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "TILEzQW74JLTTBrC",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p><em>What is the slope of the line that represents the steepness of the staircase?</em></p>"
          ]
        }
      }
    ],
